🚀 Step-by-Step Setup

1. Prerequisites

Install the following software before running the project:

Java

Check Java installation:

java -version

The project uses Java 17.

Maven

Check Maven:

mvn -version

You can also use the Maven Wrapper included in the project:

mvnw.cmd -version

MySQL

Make sure MySQL Server is installed and running.

Check that MySQL is available:

mysql -u root -p

2. Clone the Repository

Clone the GitHub repository:

git clone <YOUR-GITHUB-REPOSITORY-URL>

Move into the project directory:

cd customer-service

3. Create the MySQL Database

Open MySQL and create the database:

CREATE DATABASE customer_db;

Verify the database:

SHOW DATABASES;

Select the database:

USE customer_db;

The customer table will be created/updated automatically by Hibernate when the application starts because the project uses:

spring:
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: update

4. Configure MySQL

Open:

src/main/resources/application.yml

Configure your database connection:

server:
  port: 9090

spring:
  application:
    name: CUSTOMER-SERVICE

  datasource:
    driver-class-name: com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver
    url: jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/customer_db
    username: root
    password: "YOUR_MYSQL_PASSWORD"

  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: update
    generate-ddl: true
    show-sql: true

Important: Do not upload your real database password to GitHub. Use your own local password or environment variables.

5. Open the Project in Eclipse

Open Eclipse / Spring Tool Suite.

Select:
File → Import

Choose:
Maven → Existing Maven Projects

Select the customer-service project folder.

Click Finish.

Wait for Maven dependencies to download.

Make sure there are no errors in the project.

6. Run the Application

Open:

src/main/java/com/chn/in/CustomerServiceApplication.java

Run the application as:

Run As → Spring Boot App

The application will start on:

http://localhost:9090

7. Verify the Application

After starting the application, check the Eclipse console.

You should see that Spring Boot has started successfully and is listening on port:

9090

8. Test the Customer API

The customer controller provides the REST endpoints for customer operations.

Create Customer

POST

http://localhost:9090/customer

Example JSON:

{
  "custId": 1,
  "custName": "Prasad Chauhan",
  "custAdd": "Hyderabad"
}

You can test the API using:

Postman

Insomnia

curl

Any REST client

Example using curl:

curl -X POST http://localhost:9090/customer ^
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" ^
  -d "{\"custId\":1,\"custName\":\"Prasad Chauhan\",\"custAdd\":\"Hyderabad\"}"

9. Check Data in MySQL

Open MySQL:

USE customer_db;

Check the tables:

SHOW TABLES;

Check customer data:

SELECT * FROM customer;

The customer table contains fields corresponding to:

cust_id
cust_name
cust_add

🧩 Application Layers

Controller

CustomerController.java

Responsible for handling HTTP requests and responses.

Service

CustomerService.java

Contains the application's business logic.

Repository

CustomerRepository.java

Uses Spring Data JPA to communicate with MySQL.

Model

Customer.java

Represents the Customer database entity.

Exception Handling

The project contains:

ErrorDetails.java
GlobalExceptionHandler.java
ResourceNotFoundException.java

These classes provide centralized exception handling and meaningful error responses.

🔄 Application Flow

Client / Postman
       |
       v
CustomerController
       |
       v
CustomerService
       |
       v
CustomerRepository
       |
       v
     MySQL
       |
       v
 customer_db
       |
       v
    customer

🧪 Maven Commands

Clean the Project

mvn clean

Compile

mvn compile

Run Tests

mvn test

Package the Application

mvn clean package

Run the JAR

After packaging:

java -jar target/customer-service-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar

The exact JAR filename may differ depending on the version in pom.xml.

🌿 Git Commands

Check Git Status

git status

Add Files

git add .

Commit Changes

git commit -m "Add customer service application"

Push to GitHub

git push origin main

If your repository uses master:

git push origin master

🔐 Security Note

Never commit sensitive information such as:

MySQL passwords

API keys

Access tokens

Private keys

.env files containing secrets

Before pushing to GitHub, check:

git status

Make sure sensitive files are not being committed.

